          "answer_excerpt": "ALPHA code is MAPLE-902657.\nBETA code is RIVER-435643.\nGAMMA code is CLOUD-912485.\n\n# Advanced LLM Inference Optimization: A Technical Deep Dive\n\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) have transitioned from research curiosities to critical infrastructure components in modern software stacks. However, serving these models in production environments presents unique challenges distinct from traditional machine learning inference. Unlike classification models that process fixed-size inputs and produce fixed-size outputs, LLMs are autoregressive generators. They produce text token-by-token, where each new token depends on the entire history of previously generated tokens. This sequential dependency creates a fundamental bottleneck in throughput and latency. To address this, modern inference servers employ a suite of sophisticated optimization techniques, including continuous batching, Key-Value (KV) caching, prefix reuse, and speculative decoding. This tutorial provides a detailed technical analysis of these mechanisms, exploring their internal workings, implementation strategies, and the complex trade-offs between latency and throughput.\n\n## 1. The Fundamental Bottleneck: Autoregressive Generation\n\nTo understand why these optimizations are necessary, one must first grasp the mechanics of transformer-based inference. During the prefill phase, the model processes the entire input prompt in parallel, computing attention scores across all input tokens. This phase is compute-bound, leveraging the massive parallelism of GPUs. However, during the decode phase, the model generates one token at a time. For each new token, the model must attend to all previous tokens (the prompt plus all previously generated tokens).\n\nIf we were to recompute the attention for the entire sequence for every single new token, the computational cost would grow quadratically with the sequence length, making long-context generation prohibitively expensive. Furthermore, b